Subject: [PATCH 6/7] encoder: zero-alloc byteWriter scratch + pre-grow Marshal buffer

Two small encoder allocations show up on every Marshal hot path:

  1. byteWriter.WriteByte allocated a fresh one-byte slice on every
     call:

         _, err := bw.Write([]byte{c})

     That is one heap allocation per opcode/header byte for any
     io.Writer that does not implement io.ByteWriter. Encoding a
     small struct produced dozens of one-byte allocations for nothing.

  2. Marshal created a bytes.Buffer with zero capacity and let it
     double from 8 -> 16 -> 32 -> 64 ... for every payload. Most
     msgpack values (numbers, short strings, small structs) are well
     under 64 bytes, so the first one or two doublings were pure
     waste and an extra alloc per Marshal call.

This patch:

  * Stores a [1]byte scratch buffer on byteWriter and writes it via
    a sub-slice, so WriteByte no longer escapes to the heap. The
    wrapper is now a *byteWriter (one tiny alloc per Encoder.Reset
    instead of one per WriteByte). Concurrency is unchanged because
    each Encoder owns its own writer.

  * Pre-grows the Marshal buffer to 64 bytes so the first few
    doublings are skipped for the common small-payload case without
    over-allocating for large payloads.

No exported API or wire-format change. Output bytes are identical.
Microbenchmarks show fewer allocations per Marshal across small,
medium, and nested payloads.

Apply from the package root:

    patch -p1 < 0006-encoder-zero-alloc-byte-writer-and-pregrow-marshal.patch

---
 encode.go | 26 +++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

--- a/encode.go
+++ b/encode.go
@@ -24,18 +24,23 @@
 	WriteByte(byte) error
 }
 
+// byteWriter adapts an io.Writer that does not implement io.ByteWriter.
+//
+// The single-byte buffer is kept on the wrapper so WriteByte does not
+// allocate a fresh one-byte slice on every call. Wrappers are created
+// per Encoder.Reset, so there is no concurrency concern.
 type byteWriter struct {
 	io.Writer
+	scratch [1]byte
 }
 
-func newByteWriter(w io.Writer) byteWriter {
-	return byteWriter{
-		Writer: w,
-	}
+func newByteWriter(w io.Writer) *byteWriter {
+	return &byteWriter{Writer: w}
 }
 
-func (bw byteWriter) WriteByte(c byte) error {
-	_, err := bw.Write([]byte{c})
+func (bw *byteWriter) WriteByte(c byte) error {
+	bw.scratch[0] = c
+	_, err := bw.Write(bw.scratch[:])
 	return err
 }
 
@@ -56,11 +61,19 @@
 	encPool.Put(enc)
 }
 
+// marshalInitialBufSize is the initial backing capacity reserved by Marshal.
+//
+// Pre-growing avoids the first one or two doublings (8/16/32/64) for the
+// majority of small payloads (numbers, short strings, small structs)
+// without over-allocating for callers that encode large values.
+const marshalInitialBufSize = 64
+
 // Marshal returns the MessagePack encoding of v.
 func Marshal(v interface{}) ([]byte, error) {
 	enc := GetEncoder()
 
 	var buf bytes.Buffer
+	buf.Grow(marshalInitialBufSize)
 	enc.Reset(&buf)
 
 	err := enc.Encode(v)
